1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Høyland Gards- og ættesoge page 277 Lura (43e).
2 CONT
2 CONT To America.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/121812164/hover-hoverson-lura
2 CONT
2 CONT Hover H. Lura was the son of Hover Lura and Bertha Aslaksdatter. He immigrated to the United States from Norway in 1870.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hover H. Lura married Ellen Olena Nelson. To this union the following children are known to have been born:
2 CONT 1) Harry Neal Lura (1878-1887)
2 CONT 2) Elias Lura (Jan 1880-1968)
2 CONT 3) Inga Cordelia Lura (Mar 1883-1955)
2 CONT 4) Bertram Adolph Lura (14 Mar 1886-1951)
2 CONT 5) Harriette Lura (5 May 1888-Feb 1971)
2 CONT 6) Nettie Welhelmina Lura (Mrs. Theron T. Loomis)(18 Apr 1890-)
2 CONT 7) Harvey Elmer Lura (7 Mar 1892-1959)
2 CONT 8) Cora Jeanette Lura (Mrs. Peter Teig) (1894-1977)
2 CONT 9) Victor Eugene Lura (1898-1973)
2 CONT 10) Edna Levina Lura (6 Jun 1900-25 Mar 1989)
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT Obituary transcribed by Dena Gray-Fisher, source: Roland Record, August 2, 1917, pg. 1 [Only portion of obituary notice pertaining to Mr. Lura are included.]
2 CONT
2 CONT FIVE DEATHS WITHIN A WEEK
2 CONT
2 CONT Among This Number are Two Brothers. - Two Deaths on Monday
2 CONT
2 CONT It has become our sad duty to chronicle the death of five of our people, all of whom have passed over the Great Divide within one week.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Monday the tolling of the church bells spread the sad tidings that the Grim Reaper had entered four homes in this community and removed loved ones from each of them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Poet nor artist has never been able to portray the grave in colors of brightness and beauty. Bryant in his "Hymn of Death" could not make the subject beautiful, and yet it is the gloom of the churchyard that reveals to us more cle
2 CONC arly the beauty of life. It is the broken ties at the grave that prompts us to a full appreciation of the tenderness of the ties that are not yet broken
2 CONT ---
2 CONT H. H. Lura
2 CONT
2 CONT H. H. Lura, a prominent Howard township farmer, passed away at his home southwest of town on Monday. A stroke of apoplexy which he suffered some time ago was a contributory cause, and a cerebral hemorrhage being the immediate caus
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Lura was a native of Sandness, Stavanger, Norway, where he was born on January 14, 1851, being a son of Mr. and Mrs. Haaver Lura. He came to this country in 1870, coming directly to Story county, where several years later he l
2 CONC ocated on a farm southwest of town and which has since been his home. On March 16, 1877, he was married to Miss Ellen Nelson in this city. To this union ten children were born, one of whom died early in life. He is mourned by his 
2 CONC wife, his children - Eli, Inga, Bertram, Harriette, Nettie, Harvey, Cora, Victor and Edna - and three sisters - Bertha, Mrs. J. Evenson and Mrs. Malmin of McCallsburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Lura affiliated himself with Salem's church soon after coming here, and of which organization he has been a devoted member. he was a man who had much influence for good in the community wherein he lived. he had been honored wi
2 CONC th the position of trustee of his church, and he also took an active part in shaping the affairs of this community.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral services were conducted from the Salem's church Thursday morning by his pastor, Rev. J.N. Sandven. A large concourse of relatives and friends came to pay their respects at the last sad rites of the deceased. Interment 
2 CONC took place in the Roland cemetery.
