1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Mormon Pioneer Overland Travel 1847–1868 [http://history.lds.org/overlandtravels/pioneerDetail?lang=eng&pioneerId=36640 Horton D. Haight Company (1868)]
2 CONT
2 CONT Approximate age at departure: 28
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=39889924 Find a Grave]
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 	Sep. 17, 1839, Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 	Apr. 19, 1914 Logan Cache County Utah, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Born at Lade, Strinda, Sor Trondelag, Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Ellend Lorentzen Skanche and Jochumine Olsdatter Anzion
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Anna Christine Jacobsen, 1868
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Caroline Flygare, 10 October 1872, Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Sigrid Langaard, 25 November 1885, Salt Lake City, Salt Lake, Utah
2 CONT
2 CONT History - Anthon Lorenzo Skanchy was a noted missionary to Scandinavia. He was a native of Norway, a convert to the Church, emigrant to Utah, Bishop of the Sixth Ward in Logan, and President of the Scandinavian Mission. Born at Tr
2 CONC odhjem Norway, on Septemver 17, 1839. He was the son of Elling and Mine Ansjon Skanchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he was 21 years of age he was converted to the Mormon religion. On January 16, 1861, he was baptized at Trondhjem by Elder T. Tonnessen. He at once became active in the Church and for five years served as a local missionary i
2 CONC n his home country. Early in 1866 he was called to take charge of the Christiania Branch.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 1868, Anthon joined a group of Saints who were emigrating to America. He came to Utah Sept. 15, 1868, with the Horton D. Haight Company. On his arrival he made his home in Logan, Utah. When Cache Stake was organiz
2 CONC ed in May 1877, he was made a member of the High Council.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early in 1879 Anthon was called to undertake a mission to his native country Norway. While on this mission he baptised a widow by the name of Anna Widtsoe. She was the mother of two small sons named John and Osborne.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his return from his mission in the fall of 1881, Elder Skanchy assisted the Widtsoe family in emigrating to Utah. On April 3, 1884. John A. Widtsoe twelve years of age, was baptized by Elder Skancy "in a little creek that ra
2 CONC n below the Brighan Young College."
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Logan Sixth Ward was organized on June 6 1884, Anthon was chosen as the first Bishop.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1886 Bishop Skanchy was again called on a misison to his native country. He was first a traveling Elder and afterward was president of the Christiania Conference. He returned home in the fall of 1889.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bishop Skanchy was called for a third time to do missionary service in a foreign country when he was chosen by the First Presidency in April 1901 to preside over the Scadinavian Mission, with headquarters in Copenhagen, Denmark. W
2 CONC hile there he supervised the building of the first mission home and Chapel owned by the Church in Denmark, fist mission home and Chapel in Christiania, Norway, and purchase and remodeling of a building for the same purpose in Stoc
2 CONC kholm, Sweden. He then returned home to Utah in 1904.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bishop Sckanchy died at his home in Logan, Utah, on April 19, 1914. He had lived a useful life and was honored and respected by all who knew him. 
2 CONT
